<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merck Group</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1668">1668</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Merck KGaA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Merck is a German chemical and pharmaceutical company based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> and one of the oldest such firms in the world. It traces its origins to the Engel pharmacy in Darmstadt, acquired by the Merck family in 1668, and began industrial production of alkaloids and other chemicals in the 19th century. Its former American branch became the separate company Merck &amp; Co. after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merck-group.jpg" alt="Merck Group" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tobi Bohn Fotografie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merck_Headquarters.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The roots of Merck lie in the Landgraviate of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>. In 1668 an apothecary, Friedrich Jacob Merck, took over the Engel-Apotheke, the Angel Pharmacy, in Darmstadt. In 1816 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emanuel-merck">Emanuel Merck</a>, a descendant of the founder, took over the pharmacy. With a scientific education, he isolated and characterised several alkaloids in its laboratory and so invented a number of drugs. In 1827 he began to make these substances in bulk, offering them as a Cabinet of Pharmaceutical and Chemical Innovations. He and his successors gradually built a chemical and pharmaceutical factory that made raw materials for pharmaceutical preparations, many other chemicals and, from 1890, medicines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Wilhelm Adam Sertürner isolated morphine from opium in 1805, Merck pioneered its commercial manufacture from 1827 for an expanding world market. From 1884 it also produced and marketed cocaine, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a>, author of Über Coca (1884), collaborated enthusiastically in its cocaine research, though the method of his self-experiments has been challenged.</p><p>Vitamins became a new product line, with Vigantol introduced in 1927 and Cebion in 1934. In 1891 Georg Merck set up Merck &amp; Co. in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with Theodore Weicker; it was later confiscated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and George W. Merck, a member of an American branch of the family, repurchased its stock in 1919.</p><p>Under Nazi rule the firm was led by Karl Emanuel Merck, who joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in 1933. In the war years it made narcotics, vitamins, biocides and other chemicals. By the company's own account 265 forced labourers worked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> plant, most of them women from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, besides several hundred foreign workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. An air raid on 12 December 1944 destroyed nearly 70 per cent of the plant and killed 55 employees. After the war the military government let Merck resume making drugs, pesticides, food preservatives and laboratory chemicals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The firm was legally E. Merck oHG until 1995, when it was listed on the Frankfurt Stock Exchange and became Merck KGaA, with the family still holding a majority of the shares. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> company is the legal successor of the original Merck and holds the rights to the name in all countries except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, where Merck &amp; Co. holds them, and the two have contested the name in the courts. It marked its 350th anniversary in 2018 and calls itself the world's oldest operating chemical and pharmaceutical company.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Merck Group” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Darmstadt had a technical university and the <b>Merck</b> chemical company.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-state-of-hesse">People's State of Hesse</a></li></ul></section>
